TITLE = ("Prolonged length of stay after coronary artery bypass grafting: staged, interpretable "
         "prediction model development and temporal evaluation in a national registry")
SHORT_TITLE = "Staged prediction of prolonged stay after bypass surgery"
KEYWORDS = ("Coronary artery bypass grafting; Length of stay; Clinical prediction model; Machine learning; "
            "Explainable artificial intelligence; Temporal validation")

ABSTRACT = [
 ("bp", "Aims|Prolonged hospital stay after isolated coronary artery bypass grafting strains bed capacity. We aimed to predict it from pre-operative, intra-operative and early post-operative information, to test whether machine learning outperforms penalised regression, and to identify the main predictors and differences between patient groups."),
 ("bp", "Methods and results|Using the United Kingdom National Adult Cardiac Surgery Audit, we developed six models, including recalibrated EuroSCORE II, ridge regression with splines and gradient-boosted trees (XGBoost), in 75,814 patients operated on in 2012-2016 (15.9% of survivors stayed over 10 days) and evaluated them in 32,104 patients operated on in 2017-2019 (14.2%). XGBoost performed best, but ridge regression was within 0.004 in C-statistic at every stage. Pre-operative information alone gave a C-statistic of 0.735 (95% confidence interval 0.727-0.743); intra-operative and early post-operative information raised it only to 0.743 and 0.751, and knowing later complications gave a ceiling of 0.766. All models exceeded EuroSCORE II (0.706). Age, kidney function and urgency of surgery were the main predictors, joined by bypass time after surgery. Discrimination was lower in women (C-statistic difference -0.024) and in patients aged 80 years or over (-0.081). Over-prediction grew over time; a one-year intercept update halved it."),
 ("bp", "Conclusion|Machine learning gave no meaningful benefit over penalised regression, and most of the achievable prediction was available before surgery; registry data, which lack discharge-related factors, set a low ceiling. Lower performance in women and older patients is a potential source of inequity, and calibration drift calls for regular updating before such models support bed and discharge planning."),
]

LAY = [
 ("p", "After heart bypass surgery, most patients go home within about a week, but around one in seven stays in hospital for more than 10 days. Knowing early who is likely to stay longer would help hospitals plan beds and prepare support for going home."),
 ("p", "We used records of more than 100,000 patients who had bypass surgery in the United Kingdom. We built computer models that estimate each patient's chance of a long stay at three points: before the operation, at the end of the operation and a day or two afterwards. We then tested the models on patients treated in later years."),
 ("p", "Most of what the models could predict was already known before the operation; information from the operation and the first day or two afterwards added only a little. The models were better than the risk score that surgeons already use. However, even the best models were far from perfect, because many reasons for a long stay, such as home circumstances or bed availability, are not recorded. A simpler statistical method worked almost as well as advanced machine learning. The models relied mostly on familiar factors such as age, kidney function and how urgent the operation was. The models worked less well for women and for people aged 80 or over, which must be addressed before they are used. Because hospital stays became shorter over time, the models need regular updating to stay accurate."),
]

INTRO = [
 ("p", "Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) remains one of the most common major operations, with about 14,000 isolated procedures a year in the United Kingdom [L19]. Its recovery depends on hospital beds: in the National Health Service, a lack of staffed intensive care beds is a major cause of cancelled cardiac operations, and each extra ward or critical care bed-day carries a substantial cost [L18]. For patients, a long stay is associated with hospital-acquired infection and slower recovery of quality of life [L36, L3]. Knowing early which patients are likely to stay long would help surgical units plan beds, staffing and discharge support."),
 ("p", "Existing tools answer this question only in part. EuroSCORE II, the risk score routinely recorded in the national audit, was developed to predict in-hospital death [L17]; earlier EuroSCORE versions predicted prolonged intensive care stay only moderately [L5, L10]. The Society of Thoracic Surgeons models include prolonged stay, but define it as more than 14 days with deaths included, and were developed in the United States [L6, L29]. Most machine-learning models of length of stay after CABG come from single centres, rely on pre-operative information and are rarely tested on later patients [R41, L14, L33]; the only United Kingdom model we found was a single-centre conference report [L34]. All of these give one prediction before surgery."),
 ("p", "Yet the risk of a long stay changes as surgery and early recovery unfold. Complications drive much of the stay [L1], and adding post-operative information to pre-operative factors increased the explained variation in stay after CABG from 10% to 51% [L11]. Updating predictions at successive moments improved prediction of other surgical outcomes [L24, L25], but has not been evaluated for prolonged stay in a national registry. Three further questions remain open. First, whether machine learning outperforms traditional statistical models such as regression in cardiac surgery is disputed [L8, R18, L31]. Second, risk models drift as practice changes, as shown for the logistic EuroSCORE and for mortality models in the same national registry [L21, L22], so new models must be tested on later patients. Third, clinicians need to see what drives a prediction, but current explanation methods have known limits [L27, L28]."),
 ("p", "We therefore developed models to predict prolonged stay after isolated CABG at three moments (pre-operative; intra-operative, at the end of surgery; and early post-operative) using the National Adult Cardiac Surgery Audit, and evaluated them on later patients. We compared them with EuroSCORE II, compared regression with machine learning given the same information, estimated the ceiling of achievable accuracy, and examined which factors drive the predictions at each moment."),
]

METHODS = [
 ("h2", "Study design, data source and participants"),
 ("p", "This was a prediction model development and temporal evaluation study using the National Adult Cardiac Surgery Audit (NACSA), a prospective national registry managed by the National Institute for Cardiovascular Outcomes Research (NICOR) that records all major adult cardiac operations in the United Kingdom; its data flow and validation have been described in our previous analyses of this registry [D1-D6]. After removal of 2,992 exact duplicates, the extract comprised 377,301 records of isolated coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) performed between 1996 and March 2019 (Supplementary Methods S1)."),
 ("p", "Patients operated on in 2012-2016 formed the development cohort and those operated on in 2017-2019 the temporal evaluation cohort. Records from 1996-2011 were used only in a pre-specified comparison of training periods, because key predictors such as pre-operative creatinine were recorded for fewer than 15% of patients before 2011 (Supplementary Figures SF1-SF2). Patients who died in hospital, or whose survival status or length of stay was unknown, were excluded, leaving 75,814 development and 32,104 evaluation patients (Figure 1)."),
 ("p", "Before modelling, the data were checked for completeness by year, internal consistency and plausibility against the NICOR dataset specification; implausible values were set to missing, and derived fields containing imputed values were recalculated from source fields (Supplementary Tables ST3-ST4). The 2017-2019 records could be loaded only through a deliberate switch in the analysis code, and all modelling choices were fixed, and recorded with their reasons in a decisions log, before these data were opened."),
 ("p", "The study was approved by the Health Research Authority (HRA) and Health and Care Research Wales (HCRW) (IRAS project ID 257758) in 2020. Data were obtained from NICOR; as this was a retrospective analysis of registry data, the requirement for individual patient consent was waived. The study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, and data were handled in compliance with the UK General Data Protection Regulation."),
 ("p", "The study is reported following TRIPOD+AI [R19], RECORD [R69] and the CODE-EHR minimum standards [R68] (Supplementary Tables ST18, ST22, ST23). The protocol and statistical analysis plan were frozen on 7 October 2026, before any model was fitted to the study data [TO CONFIRM: OSF identifier]; changes made afterwards, none prompted by 2017-2019 results, are listed with their reasons in Supplementary Methods S15."),
 ("h2", "Outcome"),
 ("p", "The primary outcome was prolonged post-operative length of stay, defined as more than 10 days counted in whole days from the day of operation, among patients discharged alive. The 10-day cut-off was chosen from previous literature, in which definitions range from more than one to more than 14 days [R41]; cut-offs of 7 and 14 days, the latter used by the US Society of Thoracic Surgeons [L6], were examined in sensitivity analyses. Because survival to discharge is not known when a prediction is made, predicted risks are interpreted as the risk of prolonged stay conditional on survival; two further sensitivity analyses included patients who died in hospital (Supplementary Methods S12)."),
 ("h2", "Prediction moments and predictors"),
 ("p", "Three models were developed, one for each moment at which a prediction can be made, each using only information recorded by that moment (Figure 1). The pre-operative model used 26 predictors: demographic factors, comorbidities, cardiac status, kidney function and urgency of operation. The intra-operative model, applied at the end of surgery, added eight operative predictors (34 in total), including cardiopulmonary bypass time, myocardial protection, the number and territories of distal grafts and intra-operative intra-aortic balloon pump use. The early post-operative model added two events known within hours of surgery (36 in total): return to theatre for bleeding or tamponade and post-operative balloon pump insertion. A fourth, complication-aware model (40 predictors) added later complications (return to theatre for other cardiac reasons or for the wound, post-operative dialysis and stroke). Because these are not known at any moment when a prediction would be useful, it served only to estimate the accuracy ceiling and took no part in the stage comparison."),
 ("p", "Predictors were chosen in advance from clinical knowledge, a review of the literature on length of stay after cardiac surgery [R41, L1, L11, L14] and the registry dataset. No data-driven selection was used, because it gives optimistic performance unless repeated within every resample (Supplementary Methods S2). Definitions, coding and missingness of every predictor are given in Supplementary Table ST1."),
 ("h2", "Missing data"),
 ("p", "Missing predictor values were filled by single, deterministic chained-equation imputation written for this study, with logistic, multinomial or linear regression matched to each predictor's type; being deterministic, it gives the same patient the same risk every time, which suits a model applied to new patients one at a time [R4] (Supplementary Methods S3). Imputation was stage-ordered: pre-operative predictors were filled from pre-operative information only, operative predictors from pre-operative and operative information, and so on, so that a patient's pre-operative values are identical in every stage's model. The outcome was never used, because it is unknown when a model is applied. The imputer was fitted on the training patients of every resample and applied unchanged to held-out patients, and the final imputer, fitted on all 2012-2016 patients, was applied unchanged to 2017-2019. All models received the same imputed data, so that differences between them reflect the algorithms. Multiple imputation and complete-case analysis were sensitivity analyses (Supplementary Methods S3; Supplementary Tables ST5-ST5b)."),
 ("h2", "Models"),
 ("p", "Six models were fitted at each prediction moment. The benchmark was EuroSCORE II, recalibrated (intercept and slope) on the development data. Logistic regression with straight-line terms served as a simple comparator. Ridge-penalized logistic regression with restricted cubic splines (four knots) for every continuous predictor was the pre-specified default headline model. Three machine-learning models were fitted: XGBoost (gradient-boosted trees), random forest and a multilayer perceptron neural network. Continuous predictors were never dichotomized. No interaction terms were specified for the regressions, whereas the tree-based models and the neural network could learn interactions from the data. Input coding for each model is described in Supplementary Methods S4."),
 ("h2", "Development and internal validation"),
 ("p", "Internal performance was estimated with nested cross-validation on the 2012-2016 patients [R20]. The patients were divided once into five folds, stratified on outcome; in each round, four folds were used for tuning and fitting and the fifth was held out. Within the four folds, a single stratified 75/25 split was used for tuning. The imputer and all model inputs were refitted within every round, so that no information from held-out patients entered the models."),
 ("p", "XGBoost, random forest and the neural network were each tuned with 30 trials of Optuna's tree-structured Parzen estimator, minimizing log loss, over search spaces fixed in advance from published benchmarks (Supplementary Methods S5; Supplementary Table ST8); the ridge penalty was chosen from 30 values by the same criterion. Because tree ensembles can produce distorted probabilities [R23], the tuning of each machine-learning model also chose a post-hoc calibration step (none, Platt scaling or isotonic regression)."),
 ("p", "Finally, every model was tuned once more and fitted on all 2012-2016 patients, then saved and fingerprinted (SHA-256) before the 2017-2019 data were opened (Supplementary Methods S5)."),
 ("h2", "Choosing the headline model and judging stage gains"),
 ("p", "At each moment, ridge regression was the default headline model, because machine learning has often shown no advantage over logistic regression in clinical prediction [R18]. A machine-learning model replaced it only if, on the 2012-2016 out-of-fold predictions, its area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) was higher with the 95% confidence interval (CI) of the difference above zero, its integrated calibration index was not higher, and its net benefit was not lower at any of four key threshold risks (Supplementary Table ST9)."),
 ("p", "A later moment was judged to add value when, on 2017-2019 patients, the AUC difference between the headline models of the two moments had a 95% CI above zero and net benefit at all four thresholds (10%, 15%, 20% and 30%) was higher than that of both the earlier model and the strategies of flagging all or no patients. AUC changes were judged with bootstrap CIs rather than significance tests, which are invalid for nested models [R15]; reclassification indices were not used (Supplementary Methods S6)."),
 ("h2", "Temporal evaluation and performance measures"),
 ("p", "The locked models were applied once to the 2017-2019 patients. Discrimination was measured by the AUC; calibration by calibration-in-the-large (ideal 0), the calibration slope (ideal 1) and smoothed calibration curves with the integrated calibration index [R56]; overall accuracy by the scaled Brier score; and clinical usefulness by net benefit and decision curves between 10% and 30% threshold risk [R37]. This range was agreed with clinical co-authors because the intended use, flagging patients for bed and discharge planning, carries little harm."),
 ("p", "All 95% CIs came from 2,000 paired bootstrap resamples of patients, with differences between models or moments computed within each resample (Supplementary Methods S7). Performance was also reported by year to show drift, and a pre-specified updating scenario re-estimated the intercept (and, separately, the intercept and slope) on 2017 patients and evaluated the updated models on 2018-2019 [R32] (Supplementary Methods S7-S8)."),
 ("h2", "Explainability"),
 ("p", "The headline models were explained with exact TreeSHAP values on the log-odds scale [R53, R54] for a fixed random sample of 5,000 patients from 2017-2019, using predictors only. Importance was summarized as the mean absolute SHAP value of each predictor and, because SHAP divides credit between related predictors [R55], also of pre-specified clinical groups (Supplementary Figure SF14). Beeswarm plots show the direction and spread of each predictor's contribution for individual patients (Figure 5). As a check by an independent method, the SHAP contributions of the main continuous predictors were compared with the corresponding spline curves of the ridge regression (Figure 6); ridge odds ratios for categorical predictors are given in Supplementary Table ST13. Explanations describe what the models use for prediction, not causal effects."),
 ("h2", "Additional analyses"),
 ("p", "Six pre-specified sensitivity analyses examined the outcome definition (a composite with in-hospital death; all patients with a known stay; cut-offs of 7 and 14 days) and missing data (multiple imputation; complete cases). Ridge regression and XGBoost were also trained on 2012-2016 data with a reduced predictor set and on 1996-2016 data, to test whether older data help. Performance was compared across sex, age band and urgency of operation. Details are given in Supplementary Methods S10, S12 and S13."),
 ("h2", "Sample size"),
 ("p", "The development cohort was at least 9.7 times the minimum sample size required at every moment [R1], and the evaluation cohort 5.7 times that required for precise estimation of calibration and discrimination [R2] (Supplementary Table ST7)."),
 ("h2", "Software, data governance and use of artificial intelligence tools"),
 ("p", "Analyses used Python 3.13 with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, XGBoost, Optuna and statsmodels (versions pinned in the code repository). Every random step used fixed seeds, and every exhibit was produced by the analysis scripts from aggregate outputs. In line with the data provider's disclosure rules, counts of one to nine patients are shown as '<10' and rates based on them are not shown. Risk of bias was self-assessed with PROBAST+AI [R65] (Supplementary Table ST19)."),
 ("p", "Claude (Anthropic) [TO CONFIRM: model versions] was used, under the authors' direction, to plan and write the analysis code, run the analyses and draft text. The pseudonymised data were processed in the tool's secure cloud workspace with the approval of [TO CONFIRM: supervisor and NICOR]. Every analytic decision was made by the authors and recorded with its reasons; all code was reviewed and is publicly available; every reported number was traced to a script output; and every reference was checked against its source. The authors take full responsibility for the work."),
]

RESULTS = [
 ("h2", "Patients"),
 ("p", "After exclusions, 75,814 patients formed the development cohort and 32,104 the temporal evaluation cohort (Figure 1). Prolonged stay occurred in 12,023 (15.9%) and 4,571 (14.2%) patients, respectively. The two cohorts were similar in most characteristics (Table 1). In 2017-2019, more operations were urgent (46.8% vs 41.4%) and fewer elective (51.1% vs 56.7%), more were performed on cardiopulmonary bypass (91.8% vs 86.6%; standardized mean difference 0.17, the largest between the cohorts) and more patients had diabetes (33.8% vs 30.5%) (Table 1). The median EuroSCORE II was 1.2% in the development and 1.3% in the evaluation cohort."),
 ("h2", "Choice of headline model"),
 ("p", "On the 2012-2016 out-of-fold predictions, XGBoost met all conditions for replacing ridge regression at every moment: a higher AUC (differences 0.0033 to 0.0037, with 95% CIs above zero), a lower integrated calibration index and net benefit not lower at any key threshold (Supplementary Table ST9). Random forest also met the conditions for the intra-operative model, but with a smaller AUC gain; the neural network met them at no moment. XGBoost was therefore the headline model at all three moments."),
 ("h2", "Discrimination across prediction moments"),
 ("p", "In the 2017-2019 patients, the AUC of the headline model rose from 0.735 (95% CI 0.727 to 0.743) for the pre-operative model to 0.743 (0.735 to 0.751) for the intra-operative model and 0.751 (0.744 to 0.759) for the early post-operative model (Table 2, Figure 2); most of the achievable discrimination was therefore available before surgery. Recalibrated EuroSCORE II reached 0.706 (0.698 to 0.714), so the headline model exceeded it by 0.029, 0.037 and 0.045 at the three moments, all with 95% CIs above zero (Table 3). These temporal AUCs were within 0.002 of the internal estimates from 2012-2016 cross-validation (0.736, 0.745 and 0.753; Supplementary Table ST9), so the models showed little optimism."),
 ("p", "Both later moments added value under the pre-specified rule. The AUC increased by 0.0081 (0.0054 to 0.0108) from the pre-operative to the intra-operative model and by 0.0085 (0.0061 to 0.0109) from the intra-operative to the early post-operative model, and net benefit was higher at all four key thresholds at each step (Table 3). The net-benefit gains were small, 0.1 to 0.3 net correctly flagged prolonged stays per 100 patients per step, and for the first step the descriptive 95% CI at the 20% threshold included zero (Table 3). The same pattern held for every algorithm, except random forest between the first two moments (Supplementary Table ST11). The precision-recall AUC rose from 0.343 to 0.371, against an event rate of 14.2% (Supplementary Table ST10)."),
 ("p", "The complication-aware ceiling model, which also knew later complications, reached an AUC of 0.766 (0.758 to 0.773); all algorithms lay between 0.757 and 0.766 at this stage (Supplementary Table ST10)."),
 ("h2", "Regression and machine learning"),
 ("p", "Ridge regression with splines came close to XGBoost at every moment, with AUC differences of 0.0032 (0.0012 to 0.0053), 0.0032 (0.0013 to 0.0051) and 0.0036 (0.0018 to 0.0055) for the pre-operative, intra-operative and early post-operative models, and net benefit differed by at most 0.0014 at the key thresholds (Supplementary Table ST11). Logistic regression with straight-line terms was about 0.007 below ridge regression at every moment. Random forest and the neural network performed at or below the level of ridge regression (Table 2; Supplementary Table ST11)."),
 ("h2", "Calibration and drift"),
 ("p", "Calibration slopes were close to 1 for all models (0.999 to 1.079), but every model over-predicted the risk in 2017-2019, with calibration-in-the-large between -0.115 and -0.194 (Table 2, Figure 3). The over-prediction grew over time while discrimination did not change: for the early post-operative headline model, calibration-in-the-large was -0.106 in 2017, -0.217 in 2018 and -0.243 in 2019, with AUCs of 0.754, 0.749 and 0.750 (Supplementary Table ST15). EuroSCORE II followed the same pattern (-0.089, -0.212 and -0.273). In the pre-specified updating scenario, re-estimating only the intercept on 2017 patients roughly halved the over-prediction in 2018-2019 for this model (calibration-in-the-large from -0.222 to -0.116; integrated calibration index from 0.024 to 0.012); also updating the slope added nothing further (Supplementary Table ST12)."),
 ("h2", "Clinical usefulness"),
 ("p", "Across the 10% to 30% threshold range, every model had higher net benefit than flagging all patients or none (Figure 4; Supplementary Table ST10). For the early post-operative headline model, net benefit was 0.070, 0.050, 0.036 and 0.020 at thresholds of 10%, 15%, 20% and 30%, compared with 0.062, 0.040, 0.025 and 0.009 for EuroSCORE II (Supplementary Table ST10). When the 20% of patients with the highest predicted risk were flagged, the early post-operative headline model identified 48.6% (95% CI 47.4% to 49.9%) of all prolonged stays, with a positive predictive value of 34.6%; the figures were 46.4% and 33.0% for the pre-operative model and 41.8% and 29.8% for EuroSCORE II (Supplementary Table ST10)."),
 ("h2", "What the models use"),
 ("p", "Age was the most important predictor of the headline model at every moment, with a mean absolute SHAP value of 0.40 to 0.41 on the log-odds scale, about twice that of any other predictor (Figure 5). Before surgery, it was followed by creatinine (0.18), urgency of operation (0.17), impaired left ventricular function, diabetes and body mass index. In the intra-operative model, cardiopulmonary bypass time became the second most important predictor (0.19). Early after surgery, return to theatre for bleeding (0.067) ranked eleventh and post-operative balloon pump insertion eighteenth; both are infrequent, so their average contribution across all patients is small (Figure 5). Among the pre-specified clinical groups, kidney function (creatinine and dialysis status) contributed most at every moment (0.18) (Supplementary Figure SF14)."),
 ("p", "The ridge regression gave a consistent picture. For age, creatinine and bypass time, its spline curves and the XGBoost SHAP dependence plots had similar shapes (Figure 6). Its largest odds ratios were for return to theatre for bleeding (3.26, 95% CI 2.95 to 3.61), intra-operative balloon pump use (2.94, 2.48 to 3.48), post-operative balloon pump insertion (2.48, 2.12 to 2.89) and emergency or salvage operation (1.96, 1.70 to 2.26), and women had higher odds than men (1.48, 1.40 to 1.57) (early post-operative model; Supplementary Table ST13)."),
 ("h2", "Additional analyses"),
 ("p", "The gains across moments held for all four alternative outcome definitions, with pre-operative to early post-operative AUC gains of 0.014 to 0.023, all with 95% CIs above zero (Supplementary Table ST16). The early post-operative headline model reached AUCs of 0.729 to 0.762 under these definitions and exceeded EuroSCORE II by 0.044 to 0.048 in each (Supplementary Figure SF19)."),
 ("p", "Multiple imputation changed the AUC of the regressions by 0.0003 or less. The largest fraction of missing information was 0.18 at the three prediction moments, within the pre-specified limit of 0.21, and 0.29 for the ceiling model, which therefore received 48 imputations under the pre-specified rule. Complete cases made up 56% to 76% of development patients and 62% to 82% of evaluation patients; models developed on them differed from the main models by at most 0.004 in AUC on the same patients (Supplementary Table ST16)."),
 ("p", "Restricting 2012-2016 training to predictors recorded in every era lowered the early post-operative AUC of XGBoost from 0.751 to 0.741, and adding 1996-2011 data to this reduced set did not recover it (0.741) but increased over-prediction (calibration-in-the-large -0.311 vs -0.169) (Supplementary Table ST14, Supplementary Figure SF10)."),
 ("p", "Discrimination of the early post-operative headline model was lower in women than in men (AUC difference -0.024, 95% CI -0.044 to -0.005) and in patients aged 80 years or over than in those aged 60-69 (-0.081, -0.108 to -0.052); ridge regression and EuroSCORE II showed the same differences. Over-prediction was present in every subgroup (observed-to-expected ratio 0.84 to 0.93) except emergency or salvage operations (1.02). With a fixed top-20% flag, women, older and emergency patients were flagged more often (Supplementary Table ST17, Supplementary Figure SF13a)."),
]

DISCUSSION = [
 ("p", "In this national cohort, the prediction of prolonged stay after isolated CABG using regression and machine learning outperformed EuroSCORE II at every moment, but most of the achievable prediction was available before surgery. Intra-operative and early post-operative information met a pre-specified rule requiring both better discrimination and greater net benefit, but the gains were small, and even a model that knew later complications reached an AUC of only 0.766. XGBoost performed best, but ridge regression with splines was almost as good, so machine learning gave no meaningful benefit. All models over-predicted risk in later years, increasingly so over time, and a simple intercept update corrected much of this. The models relied mainly on patient factors, above all age, at every moment, with operative and early post-operative events adding strong but infrequent signals."),
 ("p", "The pre-operative discrimination we observed (AUC 0.735) is similar to that of the only United Kingdom model of stay after CABG we found, a single-centre pre-operative model with an AUC of about 0.71 [L34], and to that of EuroSCORE for prolonged intensive care stay (0.71) [L10]. The Society of Thoracic Surgeons model for prolonged stay reached a C-statistic of 0.78 in isolated CABG [L29], and an Ontario model reached 0.92 [L16], but these predict rarer outcomes (more than 14 days including deaths, and more than 30 days, respectively), which are easier to separate from typical recoveries and so not directly comparable."),
 ("p", "Adding information as surgery and recovery unfolded improved prediction only modestly, by about 0.008 in AUC and 0.1 to 0.3 net correctly flagged prolonged stays per 100 patients at each step. Similar staged gains have been reported for complications and costs after CABG [L24], and post-operative events explained much of the variation in stay in a large United States cohort [L11]. Our ceiling analysis suggests why the gains were small: even with later complications, discrimination rose only to 0.766. Much of what determines a long stay, such as discharge arrangements, clinician variability in discharge decisions, social circumstances, bed availability and complications not captured by the registry, is not recorded in national audit data. Machine learning performs best with granular, time-varying clinical data [L7]; richer data, not more flexible algorithms, are needed to move beyond this ceiling."),
 ("p", "XGBoost met the pre-specified rule for replacing ridge regression and was slightly better at every moment, but the margin was about 0.003 in AUC and net benefit was almost identical. This agrees with systematic reviews finding no consistent advantage of machine learning over logistic regression in clinical prediction [R18, L31], and contrasts with a meta-analysis of mortality models after cardiac surgery that found a larger advantage [L8]. Our design gave every model the same predictors and imputed data, tuned and calibrated the machine-learning models and allowed the regression curved effects; comparisons without these safeguards can favour either approach. The step from straight-line logistic regression to ridge regression with splines (about 0.007 in AUC) was larger than the step from ridge regression to XGBoost, suggesting that allowing non-linear effects matters more than the choice of algorithm. Ridge regression also offers interpretable odds ratios, simple updating and straightforward implementation, which may outweigh a small gain in discrimination."),
 ("p", "All models, including EuroSCORE II, over-predicted risk in 2017-2019, and the over-prediction grew from year to year while discrimination remained stable. The same pattern has been described for the logistic EuroSCORE in United Kingdom cardiac surgery [L21], for EuroSCORE II in the Netherlands [L23] and for machine-learning mortality models developed on the same national audit with the same time split [L22]. It is consistent with the lower rate of prolonged stay in the evaluation period (14.2% vs 15.9%), reflecting changes in practice. Re-estimating only the intercept on one year of new data halved the over-prediction in the following years, and training on older data made calibration worse rather than better. Such models should therefore be developed on recent data and recalibrated regularly, which is simple for regression and machine learning alike [R32]."),
 ("p", "The explanations were clinically recognisable. Age dominated at every moment, followed by kidney function, urgency of operation, left ventricular function, diabetes and body mass index, factors also reported by previous studies of stay after CABG [R41, L1, L12, L14]. Bypass time became the second most important predictor in the intra-operative model, reflecting operative complexity. Early complications such as return to theatre for bleeding ranked low on average because they are infrequent, yet they carried the largest odds ratios; importance averaged over all patients therefore understates factors that matter greatly for the few patients they affect, which should be kept in mind when reading importance plots."),
 ("p", "Two different methods gave the same picture: the spline curves of the ridge regression and the SHAP dependence of XGBoost had similar shapes for the main continuous predictors, suggesting that the patterns are properties of the data rather than of one algorithm. These explanations describe what the models use, not causes; they cannot show that changing a factor would shorten a stay, and credit is shared among correlated predictors [R55]. Such explanations help clinicians check that a model behaves plausibly, but do not make individual predictions more reliable [L28]."),
 ("p", "The models are intended for planning, not treatment decisions. Because most of the prediction is available before surgery, the pre-operative estimate is the most useful: it could inform scheduling and bed planning when an operation is booked; an update on arrival in intensive care could refine it, and an early post-operative estimate could trigger discharge planning on the first or second day. Flagging the 20% of patients at highest risk identified about half of all prolonged stays, with roughly one in three flagged patients staying long, a reasonable trade-off for low-harm actions such as early contact with discharge services, given that a lack of staffed intensive care beds often cancels cardiac operations [L18]. Prediction models for surgical discharge have identified avoidable bed-days when used prospectively [L32], but evidence of deployment remains scarce [L33]. Before use, our models would need re-evaluation on current data, a recalibration schedule and a prospective study of whether they change planning and patient flow."),
 ("p", "Discrimination was lower in women and in patients aged 80 years or over with every model, including EuroSCORE II, so the gap reflects the data rather than one algorithm. Women and older patients were flagged more often, in line with their higher rates of prolonged stay, but a model that ranks risk less accurately within these groups could direct planning resources to them less fairly. This potential inequity should be monitored, and group-specific calibration or thresholds considered, before any use in practice."),
 ("h2", "Strengths and limitations"),
 ("p", "The study has several strengths. It used a national registry with more than 100,000 consecutive patients and over 16,000 prolonged stays, many times the minimum sample size, and evaluated locked models on later patients rather than on a random split. The protocol was frozen before any model was fitted, every modelling choice was recorded with its reasons, and the 2017-2019 data were opened only after the models were saved and fingerprinted, with rules for choosing the headline model and judging each stage fixed in advance. The comparison of regression and machine learning was fair by design, as described above. Each prediction moment used only information available at that moment, and imputation was deterministic, so a patient's risk does not depend on a random draw. Calibration, net benefit and drift were reported alongside discrimination, an updating scenario was tested, and the main findings held in six sensitivity analyses, an alternative training period and patient subgroups. The explanations were checked against an independent method, and all code is public."),
 ("p", "The study also has limitations. The data end in March 2019, before the COVID-19 pandemic changed surgical pathways, so the models need re-evaluation on current data. The evaluation was temporal within one country, and hospital identifiers, ethnicity and deprivation were not available, so differences in discharge practice between hospitals could not be modelled and fairness could be examined only by sex, age and urgency. Social and organisational determinants of stay are not recorded in the registry, and stay ends at discharge from the operating hospital, so transfers are not captured. Patients who died in hospital were excluded, so predictions apply to patients who survive. Dichotomising stay at 10 days loses information, although the gains across moments held for 7-day and 14-day cut-offs. Finally, comorbidities left blank were treated as absent, following the registry convention, which may understate some risks."),
 ("h2", "Conclusions"),
 ("p", "In this national registry, machine learning gave no meaningful benefit over penalised regression for predicting prolonged stay after isolated CABG, although XGBoost performed best. Most of the achievable prediction was available before surgery: intra-operative and early post-operative information added little, and accuracy reached a ceiling because the registry does not capture discharge-related and social factors. Age, kidney function (creatinine), urgency of operation, left ventricular function, diabetes and body mass index were the main predictors, with bypass time added after surgery. Performance was lower in women and in patients aged 80 years or over, a potential source of inequity, and calibration drifted over the study period. Simple, regularly recalibrated models built mainly on pre-operative data could support bed and discharge planning, pending evaluation on current data and in practice."),
]

